   ('Supervisor', 'IE', "The most important thing to know is that the workshop is a place for learning, rather than a low-cost manufacturing service. Staff can demonstrate a technique and help you plan a safe sequence, but they won't complete your project while you wait. Our aim is for you to leave knowing more than when you arrived. If you need a finished item urgently, a commercial repairer may be more appropriate."),
   ('Supervisor', 'IE', "We have three rooms. The clean room is for drawing, measuring, sewing and assembling small pieces. The machine room is beyond the glass doors. You can enter it only after a member of staff has checked your training record. At the back is the finishing room, where painted or glued items can be left on marked shelves. Please don't bring food into any working room; there is a separate kitchen near the entrance."),
   ('Supervisor', 'IE', "The machines require individual introductions. Completing the general welcome session today does not qualify you to use every machine. A supervisor will show you one tool at a time, watch you use it, and then sign your record. We do that even if you have used a similar model elsewhere. Controls differ, and we need to know that you understand the equipment that is actually here."),
   ('Supervisor', 'IE', "Booking works slightly differently in each area. For a sewing machine, reserve a ninety-minute session online. The machines are popular, so please release your reservation if you cannot attend. Workbenches are not booked: you take the next available one when you arrive. If none is free, leave your name at reception and use the planning table while you wait. For painting, first ask us whether a drying shelf is available. There's no point applying paint if we have nowhere safe to leave your work."),
